ource_id\": \"s_shay_af\",\n\n  Line 182:     \"left_open\": \"Whether hundreds of pose and seed runs improve bind/no-bind agreement with threefold-axis geometry across a full capsid-by-receptor grid remains untested [s_shay_af].\",\n\n  Line 196:     {\"id\": \"s_shay_lrp6\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0\", \"quote\": \"Both AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac strongly bind human LRP6-Fc, unlike their parent capsid, AAV9, with a sub-nM apparent affinity.\", \"supports\": \"Published bind/no-bind labels for AAV9-X1.1, CAP-Mac and AAV9 against LRP6, and the LRP6 receptor in the panel.\"},\n\n  Line 197:     {\"id\": \"s_shay_af\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0/figures/4\", \"quote\": \"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1 (E1 and E2: teal, E3 and E4: yellow, AAV peptides: purple).\", \"supports\": \"Closest prior compute quantity (AlphaFold model count and recycle depth) and the E1E2-versus-E3E4 domain check used as an anchor.\"},\n\n  Line 198:     {\"id\": \"s_shay_lrp5\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0\", \"quote\": \"A pull-down assay confirmed that both AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac bind to AAVR's PKD2 domain and the full length extracellular domain of mouse LRP6 but not that of closely-related LRP5 (Supplementary Fig. 9a).\", \"supports\": \"Negative labels for LRP5 and positive labels for AAVR-PKD2 used in the reference panel and negative-control checks.\"},\n\n  Line 199:     {\"id\": \"s_shay_il3\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-52149-0\", \"quote\": \"we were able to locate the human IL3 binding site on the side of AAV9's threefold symmetry spike adjacent to the capsid's twofold symmetry valley and generate an integrative binding model.\", \"supports\": \"Engagement geometry at the threefold spike is a measured, checkable observable for capsid–partner complexes.\"},\n\n  Line 200:     {\"id\": \"s_aavr_struct\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41564-018-0356-7\", \"quote\": \"This structure reveals that of the five Ig-like polycystic kidney disease (PKD) domains in AAVR, PKD2 binds directly to the spike region of the AAV2 capsid adjacent to the icosahedral three-fold axis.\", \"supports\": \"Anchor geometry: AAVR-PKD2 engagement at the AAV2 threefold-adjacent spike, structure PDB 6IHB.\"},\n\n  Line 201:     {\"id\": \"s_aavr_review\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9950413/\", \"quote\": \"Several cryo-EM structures of AAV-AAVR complexes are published using constructs containing either two domains (PKD12) or all five PKD domains (PKD1-5) with similar results (Meyer et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2019; Silveria et al., 2020; Xu et al., 2022).\", \"supports\": \"Published depth is a handful of complexes (single digits), and the 4.5 Å contact cutoff for interface footprints.\"},\n\n  Line 202:     {\"id\": \"s_alpl\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1101/2024.03.12.584703\", \"quote\": \"We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\", \"supports\": \"ALPL in the receptor panel with a published binder (VCAP-102, ~20 nM by SPR) and AAV9 as the parent negative.\"},\n\n  Line 204:     {\"id\": \"s_phpeb\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9382559/\", \"quote\": \"We found that LY6A pulled down PHP.eB even more efficiently than PKD2 did. By contrast, LY6A did not detectably capture any AAV9 capsids, revealing significantly different receptor selectivity for AAV9 and PHP.eB.\", \"supports\": \"Receptor selectivity labels and the distinct AAVR domain usage (AAV5 uses PKD1, AAV1/2/8 use PKD2).\"},\n\n\n/home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-01/20261021-0116-loop/out/r1