    "reasoning_content": "Excellent findings so far:\n\n1. **Capsida Biotherapeutics** — NR001–NR005 BBB receptors; plate- and bead-based receptor binding detection; CAP-002 clinical trial startup; May 2026 news. The deck: \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\" and \"In Vitro Binding Platform Demonstrates Reproducible Capsid/Receptor Binding Data Consistent with Expression Profiles\". Their pipeline page shows May 2026 activity (CAP-002 program update, ASGCT data presentation). Capsida's founding tech originated in Gradinaru lab at Caltech.\n\n2. **Lir Therapeutics** (lirtx.com) — Starfish pipeline (Feb 4, 2026 blog post), nAAVigator, docking at2,3,5-fold axes. VectorBuilder partnership Aug 18-19, 2026 — very recent activity. Also CGT Catapult investment. Great \"already done\" material: they do docking of receptors onto capsid patches (\"enabling Lir to incorporate essential capsid structural analysis into large-scale viral variant library screenings\", \"It is allowing us to run docking and structural analysis tasks at scale as part of nAAVigator\").\n\n3. **Gradinaru lab (Caltech) / CLOVER Center** — Shay et al. 2024 is the closest prior. CLOVER Center — Tim Shay is \"CLOVER Director\". CLOVER = \"Center for... Lenti... \"? Actually CLOVER at Caltech: \"Caltech Laboratory of... \" Let me check. clover.caltech.edu — CLOVER Center news. The Gradinaru lab does capsid-interactor mapping: cell surface interactomes (protein microarrays), SPR, pull-down, AlphaFold-Multimer, cryo-ET. Recent activity: Brittain et al. bioRxiv June 2025 \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\". This is the structural pipeline programme.\n\nNote: the card mentions \"Lir's Starfish capsid-structure pipeline\" — Lir has a docking step using the symmetry-axis patches. Their blog says \"It is allowing us to run docking and structural analysis tasks at scale as part of nAAVigator\" (Feb 2026). And Starfish post says \"portions of the surface such as at the 2, 3 and 5-fold axes are the minimum subunit for useful docking or structural assessment of capsid stability in silico\".\n\nFor the Gradinaru/CLOVER programme — the step the card targets: building models of capsid-interactor complexes (AlphaFold-Multimer + cryo-ET integrative modeling) to locate binding sites. Quote from paper: \"Our cryo-electron tomography and AlphaFold modeling of capsid-interactor complexes reveal LRP6 and IL3 binding sites.\" or \"The complex structures of the LRP6 extracellular domain and AAV-X1 or AAV.CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptide were modeled using a cloud-based implementation of AlphaFold-Multimer-v3 provided in ColabFold v2.3.5.\" Also \"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles...\"\n\nAlso relevant: the cryo-ET step: \"we were able to locate the human IL3 binding site on the side of AAV9's threefold symmetry spike adjacent to the capsid's twofold symmetry valley and generate an integrative binding model.\"\n\nNow for \"activity\":\n- Capsida: May 2026 news (capsida.com pipeline page shows May 12, 2026 ASGCT and May 11, 2026 CAP-002 letter). Need a URL for the most recent announcement. Perhaps fetch https://www.capsida.com/pipeline or their news page. Also the deck mentions \"Clinical trial start-up activities have initiated for CAP-002\".\n- Lir: VectorBuilder partnership Aug 18, 2026 (pharmasource or vectorbuilder newsroom). Also the Starfish blog Feb 4, 2026.\n- Gradinaru/CLOVER: Brittain et al. bioRxiv June 2025 preprint. Also maybe newer. Let me check Gradinaru lab news or CLOVER news for 2026. Also check if Shay is now CLOVER Director — clover.caltech.edu. CLOVER = \"Center for... \" Let me fetch.\n\nNow \"already done\":- For Lir: Has Lir already run this experiment (hundreds of pose/seed runs, validated bind/no-bind calls against published labels at threefold sites)? Their Starfish post shows structure generation benchmarks and mentions \"LY6A docking screens\" (from the seed record quote: \"also reports 3-fold AAV9 axes within RMSD <1 A, Boltz2 OOM on an 80 GB H100 at the 5-fold axis, and LY6A docking screens\"). Let me fetch the full Starfish post to see if they validated docking calls against known binders. If they docked LY6A and got discrimination PHP.eB vs AAV9 — that would be \"partly\". Need to read the actual post.\n\n- For Gradinaru: They used 5 models per peptide — small scale. Did they do exhaustive pose enumeration? No. \"already_done: partly\" — they did the experiment in miniature (AF-Multimer models of peptides vs LRP6 domains, validated with SPR labels), but not 54-432 runs across 36 pairs.\n\n- For Capsida: They run the plate/bead binding assays experimentally, not computed binding calls. Has Capsida run computational binding-call validation? Their deck shows in vitro binding platform; their capsid engineering platform is \"unrivaled scale\" but mostly experimental + AI. \"already_done: no/unknown\" — they haven't published exhaustive computed binding-call grids against their assay labels. Note their NR001-5 receptor identity is proprietary (patented, not named publicly) - so the card's public label set is limited.\n\nLet me now fetch key pages for exact quotes:\n1. https://www.lirtx.com/post/starfish-fast-capsid-structure-generation (full text for step quote + LY6A docking + activity)\n2. https://www.lirtx.com/blog (activity dates)\n3. https://capsida.com/wp-content/uploads/Nick_Goeden_1445_May-14_Theater-C_Identification-of-Multiple-BBB-Receptors.pdf (the deck — step quotes)\n4. https://www.capsida.com/pipeline (activity: May 2026 news)\n5. https://clover.caltech.edu/news/human-cell-surface-aav-interactomes (already done / activity)\n6. Nature Comms paper for step quote (AlphaFold-Multimer modeling methods).\n7. Maybe capsida science-innovation page.\n8. VectorBuilder partnership for Lir activity.\n9. CLOVER center main page for what the center is + activity.\n\nAlso, for \"practice\" claims — need citations about how each programme handles the step today: at what scale, with which tools/people.\n\nFor Capsida practice:\n- The deck: \"Identification of Distinct Novel Receptors with High Cross-Species Conservation Significantly De-Risks Clinical Translatability ... NR001 NR002 NR003 NR004 NR005 ... NHP 96% 99% 96% 98% 93% Sequence Identity to Human\"\n- \"Capsida Biotherapeutics has successfully filed patents for all five of these groundbreaking novel receptors\"\n- \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\"\n- \"Bead-Based Receptor Binding Detection Plate-Based Receptor Binding Detection\"\n- \"In Vitro Binding Platform Demonstrates Reproducible Capsid/Receptor Binding Data Consistent with Expression Profiles\"\n\nFor Gradinaru practice:\n- Cell microarray platform to determine extracellular/cell surface interactomes\n- AlphaFold-Multimer via ColabFold v2.3.5, 5 structural models per peptide, up to 20 recycles\n- cryo-ET to locate IL3 binding site on AAV9 threefold spikeFor Lir practice:\n- Starfish: protein folding model for monomers, physics-based assembly/refinement; structure generation at 2,3,5-fold axes; scales to full capsid; \"screening tens of thousands of variants\"; docking downstream.\n- \"Starfish™ rapidly identifies relevant binding surfaces for capsid multimers using cost-efficient hardware, enabling Lir to incorporate essential capsid structural analysis into large-scale viral variant library screenings.\"\n\nNow \"if_lever_changes\" and \"if_not_changes\" for each programme:\n- If the lever is real (432 runs → balanced accuracy 0.80 vs 0.55): programmes would move from single/low-run modeling to hundreds-of-poses enumeration as standard for bind/no-bind calls; Capsida could pre-filter capsid-receptor pairs in silico before plate/bead assays; Lir would up sample counts in nAAVigator docking stage; Gradinaru lab would scale AF-Multimer pose/seed runs and trust computed calls to rank pairs for SPR/cryo-ET.\n- If not (accuracy barely moves): programmes keep small-seed modeling as hypothesis-generating only and rely on experimental binding assays; extra compute wouldn't be justified; Capsida keeps plate/bead assays as the gate; Lir wouldn't increase pose sampling for binding calls.\n\nNow I need exact quotes from pages. Let me fetch the key pages."
