s\". Capsida's step in its pipeline: in vitro receptor binding detection (plate- and bead-based) to identify which receptors bind engineered capsids. The deck slide title \"In Vitro Binding Platform Demonstrates Reproducible Capsid/Receptor Binding Data Consistent with Expression Profiles\" with sub-labels \"Bead-Based Receptor Binding Detection Plate-Based Receptor Binding Detection\" and the sentence \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\" and \"Binding also confirmed for NR002-5\" and \"Human and NHP ortholog binding confirmed\".\n\nSince the PDF fetch returned binary, I should cite the deck URL and quote from the extracted text that search retrieved. The requirement: \"copy the sentence from its own page that describes it (step_url, step_quote)\". The deck is Capsida's own hosted material. The quote \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\" describes the binding step's role. Good.\n\nAlternatively, the Chuapoco/Flytzanis/Goeden bioRxiv 2022 preprint (Capsida's platform paper) describes the integrated platform. Let me check it for a quotable description of the receptor binding assays... Actually the deck is more explicit about plate/bead formats. I'll use the deck.\n\n**Lir Therapeutics**:\n- Step: Starfish capsid-surface structure generation feeding receptor docking at symmetry axes in nAAVigator.\n- step_quote: \"Owing to its structural homogeneity, modelling the complete 60-mer AAV capsid is usually unnecessary, but portions of the surface such as at the 2, 3 and 5-fold axes are the minimum subunit for useful docking or structural assessment of capsid stability *in silico*.\" — perfect for the threefold-axis target. Or: \"Starfish™ rapidly identifies relevant binding surfaces for capsid multimers using cost-efficient hardware, enabling Lir to incorporate essential capsid structural analysis into large-scale viral variant library screenings.\"\n- The card targets exactly: receptor docking onto capsid symmetry-axis patches (2,3,5-fold) + binding calls. Lir's Applications section: \"We first showcased an application of this technology at ESGCT 2025 in Seville, demonstrating that screening of the LY6A receptor was possible by performing docking to variant capsid surfaces constructed using Starfish™.\" — this is the closest to the card's experiment (receptor docking screens). Already done status: \"partly\" — they dock LY6A to variant capsid surfaces and compare hit subsets with language-model approach, but no published bind/no-bind agreement scoring against published labels across a capsid-by-receptor grid, and no pose/seed-scale comparison (54 vs 432 runs). Also they note \"We've been using this same approach for other receptors to investigate experimental targets predicted to exhibit differential binding\" and \"We've found larger docking surfaces have been required to get relevant docking scores for screening purposes.\"\n- Activity: Blog posts through July 15, 2026 (\"The immune system doesn't want your gene therapy to work\"); VectorBuilder partnership Aug 18, 2026. Most recent activity evidence: the blog (July 15, 2026) or the VectorBuilder press release (Aug 18/19, 2026). The VectorBuilder one is more recent. Quote from pharmasource or vectorbuilder: \"VectorBuilder and Lir Therapeutics have entered a strategic collaboration to develop adeno-associated virus (AAV) capsids that address both biological performance and manufacturing requirements for gene therapy development.\" Or from the blog index: \"July 15, 2026\". Hmm, the blog index page lists posts with dates - that's good evidence of activity: \"July 15, 2026\" post. But a sentence quote is needed. I can use: \"Starfish is an AI and physics-based structure generation pipeline enabling fast, accurate and physically plausible capsid structure generation capable of large library screening tasks. It is allowing us to run docking and structural analysis tasks at scale as part of nAAVigator.\" (Feb 4, 2026 blog listing) — but for activity I want the most recent. The VectorBuilder press release Aug 18, 2026 is best. Let me fetch the VectorBuilder newsroom page or the pharmaSource page for a quote.\n\n**CLOVER Center / Gradinaru lab (Caltech)**:\n- CLOVER = \"CLARITY, Optogenetics, & Vector Engineering\" Center at Caltech. Tim Shay is CLOVER Director. The step: cell-surface interactome mapping + structural modeling of capsid–interactor complexes (AlphaFold-Multimer + cryo-ET).\n- step_quote from Nature Comms: \"Our cryo-electron tomography and AlphaFold modeling of capsid-interactor complexes reveal LRP6 and IL3 binding sites.\" — that's from the abstract (published on PubMed page). Or the methods: \"The complex structures of the LRP6 extracellular domain and AAV-X1 or AAV.CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptide were modeled using a cloud-based implementation of AlphaFold-Multimer-v3 provided in ColabFold v2.3.5.\" And fig 4c: \"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1\".\n- For \"step\" description in the programme's own words: they identify extracellular/cell-surface interactions of AAVs by cell microarray, confirm binding by SPR/pull-down, and map binding sites by cryo-ET + AlphaFold modeling.\n- Activity: Need most recent. The Gradinaru lab is active. Brittain et al. bioRxiv June 2025 \"Structural basis of liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism of CNS-targeted AAV capsids\". Also CLOVER has an AAV Repository and ASPIRE program. Need the most recent announcement/paper. Let me check clover.caltech.edu news page and gradinarulab.caltech.edu news for 2026 activity. Also maybe Shay moved on? He's \"CLOVER Director\" as of the Sept 2024 news. Let me check CLOVER people/news.\n\nAlso \"already_done\" for CLOVER: The Shay 2024 paper already ran a small version of this experiment: AF-Multimer models of capsid peptides vs LRP6 domains, checked against SPR bind/no-bind labels (AAV9-X1.1, CAP-Mac, AAV9) and cryo-ET sites (IL3 on AAV9 threefold spike). So \"partly\" — the experiment in miniature (5 models per peptide, ~6 capsids, not 36 pairs × 54-432 runs, no balanced-accuracy scoring).\n\nFor Capsida already_done: Have they run computed binding-call grids vs their plate/bead labels? Not published. Their platform is experimental screening (roboticized in vitro + NHP). \"no\" or \"unknown\". I'd say \"no\" with claim: no published exhaustive computed pose enumeration scored against their plate/bead receptor-binding labels; their validation is experimental (\"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\"). Hmm, careful: \"Check whether that programme, or work it relies on, has already run this experiment.\" For Capsida, they haven't published this. \"unknown\" might be safer since proprietary assays; but the card itself says \"NR001–NR005 and Starfish assays are proprietary\" and \"neither Capsida's nor Lir's structural pipeline has been assessed against exhaustive enumeration\". I'll say \"no\" with a claim that their public materials show experimental binding detection, not computed call validation, and no published assessment of computed binding calls against their labels. Actually \"unknown\" is more honest for proprietary work. The prompt allows yes/partly/no/unknown. For Capsida I'll use \"unknown\" — no public evidence they ran it, but their assays are proprietary. Hmm... but \"no\" + \"no public record\" is also fine. Let me think: The already_done status should say whether the programme has already run this experiment. Since Capsida's internal work is largely proprietary/unpublished, \"unknown\" is precise. But for Lir, their Starfish post shows they DID run docking screens (LY6A docking to variant capsid surfaces) — \"partly\". For CLOVER, Shay ran5 models/peptide version — \"partly\".\n\nNow \"practice\" for each (how the step is handled today: at what scale, with which tools/people) — 2-3 claims each with URL + quote.\n\nCapsida practice:\n1. \"At Capsida, the scale at which we have engineered capsids is unrivaled. Using our automated, high-throughput biological screening, our scientists assess large, diverse engineered capsid libraries for CNS and the eye in models relevant to humans...\" (science-innovation) — scale: large libraries, roboticized; tools: automated high-throughput biological screening; people: their scientists.\n2. Deck: \"In Vitro Binding Platform Demonstrates Reproducible Capsid/Receptor Binding Data Consistent with Expression Profiles\" with \"Bead-Based Receptor Binding Detection Plate-Based Receptor Binding Detection\" and \"The predictive power of in vitro receptor binding is confirmed by AAV transduction in cell lines with receptor expression\".\n3. Deck: \"Identification of Distinct Novel Receptors with High Cross-Species Conservation Significantly De-Risks Clinical Translatability\" NR001–NR005, \"Capsida Biotherapeutics has successfully filed patents for all five of these groundbreaking novel receptors\".\n4. \"We have identified novel human blood-brain barrier receptors that bind our engineered capsids and are conserved between primates and humans.\"\n\nAlso the founding origin: \"Capsida's founding technology originated in the lab of Caltech's Viviana Gradinaru, Ph.D.\" — links Capsida to the academic programme. Good for practice claim.\n\nCapsida activity: May 12, 2026 ASGCT news. Let me fetch that press release for a quote. Also \"Clinical trial start-up activities have initiated for CAP-002\" from the deck. The May 12, 2026 press release URL: https://capsida.com/capsida-to-present-preclinical-data-of-new-next-generation-high-performing-ocular-capsids-at-asgct-annual-meeting/ — fetch for a quote.\n\nLir practice:\n1. Starfish: \"The first stage of our pipeline involves applying a protein folding model to generate the monomers involved in the capsid surface. This is followed by fast physics-based assembly and refinement algorithms.\" + \"with Starfish™ able to assemble the full capsid at acceptable runtimes for screening tens of thousands of variants\" — scale.\n2. Applications: \"We first showcased an application of this technology at ESGCT 2025 in Seville, demonstrating that screening of the LY6A receptor was possible by performing docking to variant capsid surfaces constructed using Starfish™. We found a structural approach identified an alternative subset of hits compared to a language model-based approach, with the two methods being additive.\"\n3. \"We've found larger docking surfaces have been required to get relevant docking scores for screening purposes. Starfish is an important aspect of our work to both generate variants and add explainability to hits\"\n4. \"All approaches generate the 3-fold axes of AAV9 wild-type with an RMSD <1Å, but Starfish™ provides a clear advantage in runtime and scalability.\" — tools/benchmarks.\n\nLir activity: VectorBuilder partnership Aug 18, 2026 or blog July 15, 2026. Let me fetch VectorBuilder newsroom. Or use the blog index (July 15, 2026 post). I'll fetch the pharmaSource page or vectorbuilder page for a quote.\n\nCLOVER practice:\n1. Nature Comms methods/abstract: \"Here, we adapt an unbiased human cell microarray platform to determine the extracellular and cell surface interactomes of natural and engineered AAVs.\" (abstract) — their binding-readout step at scale (protein microarrays via Charles River/Retrogenix).\n2. \"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1\" — modeling scale/tools.\n3. \"The complex structures of the LRP6 extracellular domain and AAV-X1 or AAV.CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptide were modeled using a cloud-based implementation of AlphaFold-Multimer-v3 provided in ColabFold v2.3.5.\" — tools.\n4. Cryo-ET: \"we were able to locate the human IL3 binding site on the side of AAV9's threefold symmetry spike adjacent to the capsid's twofold symmetry valley and generate an integrative binding model.\"\n\nCLOVER activity: Need something recent (2025-2026). The PubMed related citations show Brittain et al. bioRxiv June 2025. Also the CLOVER website copyright 2026 and ASPIRE program. Let me fetch clover.caltech.edu news and/or Publications page for a2026 item. Also gradinarulab.caltech.edu.\n\nLet me also confirm the Nature Comms abstract sentence for step quote and get the exact cryo-ET sentence from the paper page.\n\nAlso, I should double check \"already done\" for Lir more carefully — the card's experiment: 36 capsid-receptor pairs, docking + AF-Multimer seeds + MD relaxations, scored on published bind/no-bind labels with threefold-site geometry. Lir has done LY6A docking screens on variant capsid surfaces (Starfish-built), compared hits to language model approach. They haven't published bind/no-bind call accuracy against published labels across a grid, nor a compute-scaling (54→432) comparison. So \"partly\".\n\nOne more candidate to consider: the \"Voyager/ALPL VCAP-102\" programme (Moyer et al. 2025) — they identified ALPL as the BBB receptor for VCAP-102. But that's a single-receptor mapping study, and the card's result would feed them... but they're more about receptor identification than computed binding calls. The three picks I have (Capsida, Lir, CLOVER/Gradinaru) are the strongest and are named in the option + closest prior.\n\nWait — one consideration: is \"CLOVER Center\" the right name or \"Gradinaru lab\"? The prompt wants \"named labs, companies, open-source projects or consortia\". The Shay et al. work was led by \"CLOVER Director Tim Shay\" per CLOVER news — CLOVER Center at Caltech (CLARITY, Optogenetics, & Vector Engineering). Gradinaru is the PI of CLOVER presumably. Let me check the CLOVER about/people page to name it correctly. Actually the paper's last author is Viviana Gradinaru. The CLOVER center is the programme. I'll name it \"CLOVER Center (Caltech) — Gradinaru laboratory\" or just \"CLOVER Center, Caltech (Gradinaru lab)\". Let me fetch clover.caltech.edu home + people to get the right framing and a recent activity item.\n\nAlso for the card's check-first anchor (PDB 6IHB, AAVR-PKD2 on AAV2 threefold spike) — that's from Zhang et al. 2019 (a different lab). Not needed as a programme.\n\nNow, \"if_lever_changes\" / \"if_not_changes\" per programme:\n\nCapsida:\n- if_lever (balanced accuracy 0.80 at 432 runs vs 0.55 at 54, 5/6 binders at published sites): Capsida could add a computed bind/no-bind gate before plate/bead assays, reducing the capsid-receptor pairs taken into wet-lab validation; their \"predictive power of in vitro receptor binding\" chain would extend upstream to in silico pose enumeration at ~hundreds of runs per pair; the NR001–NR005 receptor-binding format would get computed calls as a pre-filter.\n- if_not (accuracy moves <0.05, ≤3/6 binders reach sites): no change to their practice — plate- and bead-based detection remains the binding gate; extra compute would not be added; the computed calls wouldn't be trusted to prioritize pairs.\n\nLir:\n- if_lever: nAAVigator's docking stage would move from modest pose sampling to hundreds of pose/seed/MD runs per capsid–receptor pair as the validated way to emit bind/no-bind calls; their threefold-axis surface construction would become the validated binding-site criterion; they'd budget compute accordingly in variant screens.\n- if_not: keep single-pass/low-sample docking as a ranking heuristic only (not a binding call); rely on lab-in-the-loop experimental validation; their note \"larger docking surfaces have been required to get relevant docking scores\" would stand as the current tuning knob rather than run count.\n\nCLOVER:\n- if_lever: their AlphaFold-Multimer + cryo-ET mapping step would scale from 5 models per peptide to hundreds of pose/seed runs as standard for calling bind/no-bind and sites across a capsid-by-receptor grid; they could prioritize which pairs to take to SPR/cryo-ET by computed calls.\n- if_not: keep AF-Multimer as illustrative model generation (\"Representative AlphaFold models...\") and rely on cell microarray/SPR/pull-down/cryo-ET for calls; no compute scaling.\n\nalready_done:\n- Capsida: unknown — their public materials show wet-lab binding platforms; no public record of exhaustive computed binding-call validation; assays proprietary.\n- Lir: partly — LY6A docking screens on Starfish surfaces at ESGCT 2025, compared to LM hits; no published balanced-accuracy validation against published bind/no-bind labels or run-count scaling.\n- CLOVER: partly — Shay et al. 2024 ran AF-Multimer (5 models/peptide) against SPR labels and cryo-ET sites for a handful of capsids; not the full grid or hundreds of runs.\n\nNow I need:\n1. Fetch Capsida May 12 2026 press release for activity quote.\n2. Fetch VectorBuilder newsroom / pharmaSource for Lir Aug 2026 activity quote.\n3. Fetch CLOVER news/publications for recent activity (2025/2026).\n4. Get exact quotes from Nature Comms page for the step (I have from search: abstract sentence and methods sentence — good enough; but let me fetch the paper page to copy exact sentences).\n5. Maybe fetch the ESGCT 2025 Lir poster? Dropbox link — the Starfish page quotes its content already (\"demonstrating that screening of the LY6A receptor was possible by performing docking to variant capsid surfaces constructed using Starfish™\").\n\nLet me do these fetches now."
