uttle targets CA-IV for potent brain delivery\"; also April 13, 2026 \"Novel BBB regulators verified with AAV9-X1.1\"; March 30, 2026 \"Structural basis of AAV liver de-targeting and neuronal tropism\". These are great for the CLOVER/Gradinaru activity + practice (they continue receptor/binding mapping work with AAV9-X1.1). Let me fetch the April 13, 2026 \"Novel BBB regulators verified with AAV9-X1.1\" news item — it may describe their current receptor-verification pipeline. And the March 30, 2026 structural basis one. These would give practice quotes for how they handle binding/site mapping now.\n\nLet me fetch those two CLOVER news items. Also maybe the Nature Communications article page for exact sentences (abstract + methods). I have good excerpts from search already for the Nature paper:\n- Abstract: \"Our cryo-electron tomography and AlphaFold modeling of capsid-interactor complexes reveal LRP6 and IL3 binding sites.\"\n- \"Here, we adapt an unbiased human cell microarray platform to determine the extracellular and cell surface interactomes of natural and engineered AAVs.\"\n- Methods: \"The complex structures of the LRP6 extracellular domain and AAV-X1 or AAV.CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptide were modeled using a cloud-based implementation of AlphaFold-Multimer-v3 provided in ColabFold v2.3.5.\"\n- Fig 4c: \"Representative AlphaFold models, from 5 structural models each of X1 and CAP-Mac peptides with up to 20 recycles, predict selective interaction with human LRP6 domain E1\"\n\nLet me fetch the Nature paper page directly to confirm quotes (or use PMC version which is more fetchable: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11381518/). Also fetch the two CLOVER news pages."
