low, AAV peptides: purple). **d** SPR of mouse LRP6-E1E2 and LRP6-E3E4 (the minimal stable extracellular domain fragments due to cooperative folding) confirms that AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac bind only to LRP6-E1E2. Panel a created with BioRender.com released under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivs 4.0 International license.\n\n  Line 217: LRP6 has many endogenous Wnt signaling partners with binding sites spanning either extracellular YWTD domains 1 and 2 (E1E2) or domains 3 and 4 (E3E4)[51](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR51 \"Ren, Q., Chen, J. & Liu, Y. LRP5 and LRP6 in Wnt signaling: Similarity and divergence. Front. Cell Dev. Biol. 9, 670960 (2021).\"). We applied AlphaFold-Multimer[52](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR52 \"Evans, R. et al. Protein Complex Prediction with AlphaFold-Multimer. \n\n  Line 259: Engineered capsid-receptor complexes have proven challenging targets for traditional structural biology techniques. However, using a combination of biophysical techniques including cryo-ET sub-tomogram averaging with symmetry expansion, we were able to locate the human IL3 binding site on the side of AAV9’s threefold symmetry spike adjacent to the capsid’s twofold symmetry valley and generate an integrative binding model. Sub-tomogram averaging is well suited to the low affinity, high avidity and dynamism of receptor interactions with engineered capsids and we expect the method to have broad utility alongside single particle techniques. Importantly, the implications of the IL3 interaction cannot be readily studied in mice as AAV9, an isolate from human clinical tissue[65](/articles/s41467-024-52149-0#ref-CR65 \"Gao, G. et al. Clades of adeno-associated viruses are widely disseminated in human tissues. J. Virol. 78, 6381–6388 (2004).\"), binds to human and macaque (83% AA identity) but not marmoset or mouse IL3 (69% and 27% AA identity, respectively). It is possible that this species-dependent interaction could contribute to a disconnect bet