    "content": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_e59c66fc4fb6dea49b55285875f99f32\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE\",\n      \"title\": \"GitHub - GradinaruLab/APPRAISE: Rank binders by structure modeling · GitHub\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"GradinaruLab/APPRAISE\\n* Page: GitHub repository\\n* URL: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE\\n* Description: Rank binders by structure modeling\\n* Stars: 17\\n* Forks: 7\\n* Default branch: main\\n* Created: 2023-01-12T00:59:38.000Z\\n* Commits: 213\\nTop-level files\\nA utomated P air-wise P eptide- R eceptor binding model A nalys I s for S creening E ngineered proteins ( APPRAISE ) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\\nget_complex_fastas(\\nreceptor_name=\\\"LY6A\\\",\\nreceptor_seq=\\\"...\\\",\\nlist_peptide1_names=[\\\"PHP.eB\\\", \\\"AAV9\\\"],\\nlist_peptide1_seqs=[\\\"...\\\", \\\"...\\\"],\\nmode=\\\"pairwise\\\",\\nmodeling_backend=\\\"boltz1\\\",\\nfolder_path=\\\"./boltz_inputs/\\\",\\n)\\nUsing newer modeling backends from Colab-APPRAISE\\nManuscript\\nXiaozhe Ding*, Xinhong Chen, Erin E. Sullivan, Timothy F Shay, Viviana Gradinaru*. APPRAISE: Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structural modeling. Molecular Therapy (2024). * Corresponding authors.\\nManuscript-related data\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.11.523680v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structural modeling\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-01-14\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Here we bridge this gap by introducing Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a method for predicting the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins.\\nAfter generating models of engineered proteins competing for binding to a target using an established structure-prediction tool such as AlphaFold2-multimer or ESMFold, APPRAISE performs a rapid (under 1 CPU second per model) scoring analysis that takes into account biophysical and geometrical constraints.\\nIntroduction\\nTo bridge the remaining gap between structure prediction and protein engineering, here we present Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a readily interpretable and generalizable method for ranking the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on competitive structure modeling and\\nfast physics-informed structure analysis.\\nMethods\\nAPPRAISE Methodology\\nThe code and demos for APPRAISE are available through a public Github repository: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE.\\nModeling of peptide-receptor complexes using AF2-multimer\\nAutomated quantification of the peptide-receptor models\\nXu, G.; Zhang, R.; Li, H.; Yin, K.; Ma, X.; Lou, Z. Structural basis for the neurotropic AAV9 and the engineered AAVPHP.eB recognition with cellular receptors. Molecular Therapy - Methods Clinical Development 2022, 26, 52–60.\\n40. Jang, S.; Shen, H. K.; Ding, X.; Miles, T. F.; Gradinaru, V.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.11.523680v2\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structure modeling\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-10-16\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Here we bridge this gap by introducing Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a method for predicting the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins.\\nAfter generating models of engineered proteins competing for binding to a target using an established structure-prediction tool such as AlphaFold-Multimer or ESM-Fold, APPRAISE performs a rapid (under 1 CPU second per model) scoring analysis that takes into account biophysical and geometrical constraints.\\nTo bridge the remaining gap between structure prediction and protein engineering, here we present Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a readily interpretable and generalizable method for ranking the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on competitive structure modeling and\\nfast physics-informed structure analysis.\\nAutomated quantification of the peptide-receptor models\\nAll code is available in a GitHub repository:\\ngithub.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE\\nIn addition, APPRAISE is made accessible through a web-based notebook interface using Google Colaboratory. The notebook can be found in the GitHub repository above or be directly accessed through the link:\\ntiny.cc/APPRAISE\\nStructural basis for the neurotropic AAV9 and the engineered AAVPHP.eB recognition with cellular receptors. Molecular Therapy - Methods Clinical Development 26, 52–60 (2022). URL https://doi.org/10.1016/j.omtm.2022.05.009.\\n41. Jang, S., Shen, H. K., Ding, X., Miles, T. F. & Gradinaru, V.\\nStructural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB. Molecular Therapy - Methods Clinical Development 26, 343–354 (2022). URL https://doi.org/10.1016/j.omtm.2022.07.011.\\n42. Morrone, J. A., Perez, A., MacCallum, J. & Dill, K. A. Computed Binding of Peptides to Proteins with MELD-Accelerated Molecular Dynamics.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pypi.org/project/appraise/1.2.6\",\n      \"title\": \"appraise · PyPI\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A utomated P air-wise P eptide- R eceptor binding model A nalys I s for S creening E ngineered proteins ( APPRAISE ) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\\nget_complex_fastas (\\nreceptor_name = \\\"LY6A\\\" ,\\nreceptor_seq = \\\"...\\\" ,\\nlist_peptide1_names = [ \\\"PHP.eB\\\" , \\\"AAV9\\\" ],\\nlist_peptide1_seqs = [ \\\"...\\\" , \\\"...\\\" ],\\nmode = \\\"pairwise\\\" ,\\nmodeling_backend = \\\"boltz1\\\" ,\\nfolder_path = \\\"./boltz_inputs/\\\" ,\\n)\\nUsing newer modeling backends from Colab-APPRAISE\\nWhen you run Boltz-1, Chai-1, or OpenFold3 outside APPRAISE, keep the APPRAISE job name as the input file stem. APPRAISE uses that stem to match structure outputs back to peptide competitions during quantification.\\nIf you start from APPRAISE-generated Step 1 inputs, no additional renaming is usually required.\\nManuscript\\nXiaozhe Ding*, Xinhong Chen, Erin E. Sullivan, Timothy F Shay, Viviana Gradinaru*. APPRAISE: Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structural modeling. Molecular Therapy (2024). * Corresponding authors.\\nManuscript-related data\\nThe dataset contains all structural models and sequences used in Ding et al., 2024.\\nGithub repository\\nThe repository contains the latest version of APPRAISE package, Colab-APPRAISE notebook, and demo notebooks.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pypi.org/project/appraise/1.2/\",\n      \"title\": \"appraise · PyPI\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A utomated P air-wise P eptide- R eceptor binding model A nalys I s for S creening E ngineered proteins ( APPRAISE ) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\\nManuscript:\\nXiaozhe Ding*, Xinhong Chen, Erin E. Sullivan, Timothy F Shay, Viviana Gradinaru*. APPRAISE: Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structural modeling. BioRxiv (2023). * Corresponding authors.\\nAPPRAISE Github repository\\n(The repository contains the latest version of APPRAISE package and demo notebooks.)\\nColabFold Github repository\\n(ColabFold provides a panel of user-friendly tools for structure modeling that are used by APPRAISE.)\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pypi.org/project/appraise/1.2.1/\",\n      \"title\": \"appraise · PyPI\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"A utomated P air-wise P eptide- R eceptor binding model A nalys I s for S creening E ngineered proteins ( APPRAISE ) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\\nManuscript:\\nXiaozhe Ding*, Xinhong Chen, Erin E. Sullivan, Timothy F Shay, Viviana Gradinaru*. APPRAISE: Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structural modeling. BioRxiv (2023). * Corresponding authors.\\nAPPRAISE Github repository\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.11.523680v2.full-text\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by receptor binding propensity using structure modeling | bioRxiv\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-01-01\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"View ORCID Profile Xiaozhe Ding , View ORCID Profile Xinhong Chen , View ORCID Profile Erin E. Sullivan , View ORCID Profile Timothy F. Shay , View ORCID Profile Viviana Gradinaru\\ndoi: https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.01.11.523680\\nAbstract\\nHere we bridge this gap by introducing Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a method for predicting the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins.\\nAfter generating models of engineered proteins competing for binding to a target using an established structure-prediction tool such as AlphaFold-Multimer or ESM-Fold, APPRAISE performs a rapid (under 1 CPU second per model) scoring analysis that takes into account biophysical and geometrical constraints.\\nIntroduction\\nTo bridge the remaining gap between structure prediction and protein engineering, here we present Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a readily interpretable and generalizable method for ranking the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on competitive structure modeling and\\nfast physics-informed structure analysis.\\n* Download figure\\n* Open in new tab\\nFig. 1 Workflow of Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE).\\nFirst, peptides from the engineered protein candidates’ receptor-binding region are modeled in competing pairs with the target receptor using tools like AF-Multimer or ESMFold.\\nWe first developed APPRAISE to predict the binding propensities of engineered Adeno-Associated Viral (AAV) capsids for brain receptors. Recombinant AAVs are widely used as delivery vectors for gene therapy due to their relative safety as well as their broad and engineerable tropism.\\nAutomated quantification of the peptide-receptor models\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(24)00219-3\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by target-binding propensity using structure modeling: Molecular Therapy\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-06-05\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Division of Biology and Biological Engineering, California Institute of Technology, 1200 E California, Boulevard, Pasadena, CA 91125, USA\\nSearch for articles by this author ∙ Viviana Gradinaru Viviana Gradinaru 0000-0001-5868-348X\\nCorrespondence\\nCorresponding author: Viviana Gradinaru, Division of Biology and Biological Engineering, California Institute of Technology, 1200 E California, Boulevard, Pasadena, CA 91125, USA.\\nviviana@caltech.edu\\nAffiliations\\nDivision of Biology and Biological Engineering, California Institute of Technology, 1200 E California, Boulevard, Pasadena, CA 91125, USA\\nTo bridge this gap, we introduce Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a method for predicting the target-binding propensity of engineered proteins.\\nAfter generating structural models of engineered proteins competing for binding to a target using an established structure prediction tool such as AlphaFold-Multimer or ESMFold, APPRAISE performs a rapid (under 1 CPU second per model) scoring analysis that takes into account biophysical and geometrical constraints.\\nTo bridge the remaining gap between structure prediction and protein engineering, here we present Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a readily interpretable and generalizable method for ranking the target-binding propensity of engineered proteins based on competitive structure modeling and\\nfast physics-informed structure analysis.\\nThe workflow of APPRAISE ( Figure 1 ) comprises four main components. In the first step, pairs of peptides from 𝑁 candidate protein molecules ( 𝑁 2 pairs total) are modeled in complex with a target receptor using a state-of-the-art structure method such as AF-Multimer. 14 14.\\nEvans, R. ∙ O’Neill, M. ∙ Pritzel, A. ...\\nHere, we describe APPRAISE, a structure-based, physics-informed method that accurately ranks target-binding propensities of engineered proteins. APPRAISE uses a competitive, pairwise modeling strategy to capture affinity differences between even proteins with similar sequences and takes into account biophysical and geometrical principles.\\nAutomated quantification of the peptide-receptor models\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.01.12.523632v1\",\n      \"title\": \"Primate-conserved Carbonic Anhydrase IV and murine-restricted Ly6c1 are new targets for crossing the blood-brain barrier\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2023-01-13\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"To demonstrate how a known target can unlock new engineering strategies, (1) we created an enhanced Ly6c1-binding AAV variant, AAV-PHP.eC, (2) we utilized a new method for Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered AAVs (APPRAISE-AAV for short) that uses AlphaFold2(42) to screen peptides against potential receptors in\\nWe began by applying an AlphaFold2-based computational method(42, 52) for Automated Pairwise Peptide Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered AAV (APPRAISE-AAV for short).\\nAutomated Pairwise Peptide Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered AAVs (APPRAISE-AAV)\\nYin, X. Ma, Z. Lou, Structural basis for the neurotropic AAV9 and the engineered AAVPHP.eB recognition with cellular receptors. Molecular Therapy - Methods & Clinical Development 26, 52–60 (2022).\\n57. S. Jang, H. K. Shen, X. Ding, T. F. Miles, V. Gradinaru, Structural basis of receptor usage by the engineered capsid AAV-PHP.eB.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/nyxge-gz849\",\n      \"title\": \"Fast, accurate ranking of engineered proteins by target-binding propensity using structure modeling | CaltechAUTHORS\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2024-04-06\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"* Ding, Xiaozhe ORCID icon\\n* Chen, Xinhong\\n* Sullivan, Erin E.\\n* Shay, Timothy F. 1 ORCID icon\\n* Gradinaru, Viviana 1 ORCID icon\\n  Show affiliations\\n  1. ROR icon California Institute of Technology\\nAbstract\\nTo bridge this gap, we introduce Automated Pairwise Peptide-Receptor Analysis for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE), a method for predicting the target-binding propensity of engineered proteins.\\nAfter generating structural models of engineered proteins competing for binding to a target using an established structure prediction tool such as AlphaFold-Multimer or ESMFold, APPRAISE performs a rapid (under 1 CPU second per model) scoring analysis that takes into account biophysical and geometrical constraints.\\nAs proof-of-concept cases, we demonstrate that APPRAISE can accurately classify receptor-dependent vs.\\nreceptor-independent adeno-associated viral vectors and diverse classes of engineered proteins such as miniproteins targeting the severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) spike, nanobodies targeting a G-protein-coupled receptor, and peptides that specifically bind to transferrin receptor or programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1).\\nContributions\\nX.D. and V.G. conceived the project. X.D. developed the APPRAISE method, applied APPRAISE to rank the engineered protein binders, and analyzed the results. X.C. identified PHP.D through directed evolution and characterized PHP.D in vivo . E.E.S. and T.F.S. designed and performed in vitro infectivity assays.\\nConflict of Interest\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"warnings\": null,\n  \"metadata\": null,\n  \"session_id\": \"ses_ea3641f24ffeIOZGZcPjtin4s1\"\n}"
